Lost STREAM data resent frame by frame, as quic-go resends it.

quic-go's OnLost puts a lost STREAM frame, whole, at the back of its stream's retransmissionQueue
(vendor/quic-go/send_stream.go:897-941), and its framer sends the queue in that order while 128
bytes of the packet are left (vendor/quic-go/framer.go:140-176): each frame on its own, or when it
does not fit what MaybeSplitOffFrame cuts from its front (vendor/quic-go/send_stream.go:508-532,
vendor/quic-go/internal/wire/stream_frame.go:161-187), asked with the Length field's length added
to the room, the rest staying at the head; a frame of which not a byte fits ends the
retransmissions of that packet.

Upstream ngtcp2 kept lost data by offset (a skip list), resent the lowest first, merged lost
ranges that met into one frame, left out bytes a late ACK had covered, and dropped a lost frame
whose bytes were all covered so.

Now an application stream keeps its lost frames in strm->tx.lc_rtxq_head/lc_rtxq_tail, first lost
first; ngtcp2_strm_lc_rtxq_pop takes them as popRetransmissionFrame does, and conn_write_pkt asks
for them as the framer does, writing the frame without its Length when it only fits so (it then
fills the packet and is last, see 0022). rtb_reclaim_frame gives every lost frame of a stream not
reset back to it. CRYPTO data keeps ngtcp2's skip list: its layout is 0005 and 0014's.

The frames are inside the encryption, for the node alone to see; the packets are not, and one that
carries resent data and is not full can now be a few bytes longer or shorter than ngtcp2 made it —
the size quic-go's would have.

Checked in test/ngpatch: the order of the queue, the cut against quic-go's MaxDataLen on 1164
cases (tools/qgovec), a frame of which nothing fits, two lost frames that meet going out as two
even when bytes of one were acknowledged meanwhile, a lost frame cut to fill a packet exactly,
and the 128-byte floor at 128 and 127.

